# Jittered positions: position_jitter

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()
mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`position_jitter()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/position_jitter.html), in the order
the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly
cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a
list of what remains.

`position_jitter()` adds a little random noise to each position, which can make a small dataset
easier to read when at least one of its positions is discrete: points that would sit on top of one
another spread apart. The noise goes both ways, by up to 40% of the data's resolution unless `width`
and `height` say otherwise, and a `seed` makes it the same every time. Giggly has
`position: :jitter`, or `{:jitter, options}` with `width:`, `height:` and `seed:`, which
`Giggly.Plot.geom_point/3`'s `position:` takes, and `Giggly.Plot.geom_jitter/3`, which jitters its
points with it.

Giggly's jitter draws Erlang's random numbers, not R's, so its points land elsewhere than ggplot2's,
each within the same distance of its place, and an axis that widens to hold them can end a little
elsewhere too, by no more than about twice that distance; everything else in a drawing is ggplot2's.
Where ggplot2 draws new numbers each time a plot is drawn unless given a `seed`, Giggly draws the
same points every time, from a seed of 0 unless `seed:` gives another, and Giggly's `seed: 0` draws
other numbers than ggplot2's `seed = 0`.

A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The examples draw
`Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999
to 2008, and `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US
magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mpg` and `mtcars`, their names in R.

## Jittered points beside boxplots

```r
# Jittering is useful when you have a discrete position, and a relatively
# small number of points
# take up as much space as a boxplot or a bar
ggplot(mpg, aes(class, hwy)) +
  geom_boxplot(colour = "grey50") +
  geom_jitter()
```

`geom_jitter()` is a shortcut for `geom_point(position = "jitter")`. The boxes' outline is
`"grey50"`, a name only R has, written as its hex, `"#7F7F7F"`.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :class, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_boxplot(colour: "#7F7F7F")
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> render.()
```

## Too much jitter

```r
# If the default jittering is too much, as in this plot:
ggplot(mtcars, aes(am, vs)) +
  geom_jitter()
```

`am` and `vs` are each 0 or 1, so unjittered points would sit on top of one another.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :am, y: :vs})
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> render.()
```

## Less jitter

```r
# You can adjust it in two ways
ggplot(mtcars, aes(am, vs)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.1, height = 0.1)
ggplot(mtcars, aes(am, vs)) +
  geom_jitter(position = position_jitter(width = 0.1, height = 0.1))
```

The two draw the same points in Giggly, as both draw from a seed of 0, where ggplot2's each draw
new ones.

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :am, y: :vs})
|> Plot.geom_jitter(width: 0.1, height: 0.1)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :am, y: :vs})
|> Plot.geom_jitter(position: {:jitter, width: 0.1, height: 0.1})
|> render.()
```

## Reproducible jitter

```r
# Create a jitter object for reproducible jitter:
jitter <- position_jitter(width = 0.1, height = 0.1, seed = 0)
ggplot(mtcars, aes(am, vs)) +
  geom_point(position = jitter) +
  geom_point(position = jitter, color = "red", aes(am + 0.2, vs + 0.2))
```

With a `seed`, the position jitters both layers the same way, so each red point sits 0.2 above
and to the right of its black one. Giggly writes `am + 0.2` as `{&Kernel.+/2, [:am, 0.2]}`.

```elixir
jitter = {:jitter, width: 0.1, height: 0.1, seed: 0}

mtcars
|> Plot.new(%{x: :am, y: :vs})
|> Plot.geom_point(position: jitter)
|> Plot.geom_point(%{x: {&Kernel.+/2, [:am, 0.2]}, y: {&Kernel.+/2, [:vs, 0.2]}},
  position: jitter,
  colour: "red"
)
|> render.()
```